--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Proposed documentation structure (reasoned, not assumed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Precedent observed: shared/infra modules with no distinct external
audience (`regression-core`) stay documented centrally in the top-level
README; product modules with their own configuration/execution concerns
(`regression-petstore-api`, `regression-jhipster`, `regression-nextjs-commerce`)
each get their own README.

`regression-mcp-server` does not fit either precedent cleanly: it is
architecturally isolated infra (like `regression-core`), but it has a
genuinely distinct external audience — someone installing/configuring it as
an MCP client in IDEA or Codex, who is not the same reader as someone
skimming the top-level README for "what modules does this reactor have," and
is also not CLAUDE.md's audience (a coding agent editing this repository's
source). The top-level README's current single paragraph-cluster is already
straining under Stage 14/15 content growth (~4 dense paragraphs covering
lifecycle, security, run store, artifacts, and tool inventory all at once).

Recommendation: give `regression-mcp-server` its own
`regression-mcp-server/README.md`, matching the pattern already used by the
three product modules, and move the full v1.0 documentation set there:
installation, IDEA/Codex configuration, tools and schemas, security model,
execution lifecycle, run store, artifact limits, JAR-lock troubleshooting,
v1.0 limitations. The top-level README.md's "MCP inspection server" section
would then shrink to a short summary + pointer (mirroring how the
top-level README already handles the three product modules via its
"Further reading" list), rather than continuing to grow in place.
CLAUDE.md's existing "Regression MCP server" section needs no change — its
audience and purpose (agent-facing repository rules) are already correctly
scoped and distinct from end-user installation/configuration docs; the two
should stay separate, not merged.

One open, genuinely tactical (not architectural) sub-question, flagged
rather than silently decided: whether "tools and schemas" is large enough
to warrant splitting into its own file (e.g.
`regression-mcp-server/docs/TOOLS.md`) once written, given 14 tools each
with input/output JSON Schema, versus keeping it as one section of the new
README.md like every other topic. Default recommendation is to keep it as
one section of the new README.md unless drafting it turns out to make the
file unwieldy — no other module in this repository splits its docs across
multiple files today, so a single new README.md is the more consistent
choice absent a concrete size problem. This is a judgment call, not a
locked decision — happy to split if preferred.

Nothing above was written yet — this is the structural proposal only,
Phase A.

